    {
      "turn": 2,
      "doctor_question": "It sounds like you have been feeling under a lot of stress with everything going on. Have you ever heard any voices or sounds when no one else was around?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S020",
      "patient_response": "Well, yes, actually. And it's funny you bring that up because my husband, he never hears it, which just makes him think I'm more crazy, but I know what I hear. It usually happens when I'm finally home from work, you know, after I've put away all my cleaning supplies—the bleach, the rags, the mop—and I'm trying to get dinner started or maybe just wiping down the counters one last time. The house will be completely quiet, just dead silent, especially if my husband is resting in the living room. \n\nAnd then, right by the kitchen window—the one that looks out over the side yard where the hydrangeas are—I'll hear her. It's a woman's voice. And she doesn't just mumble, doctor. It's not like a whisper or the wind or something you could just brush off. It is clear as day, as if she is standing right there on the other side of the glass, or maybe even right inside the kitchen with me. She'll tell me things I need to do. She'll say, \"Check the locks,\" or she'll tell me to move his pills to a different spot. And I have to listen, because what if they're trying to get in? What if the agency people sent her to test me? So I go and check the deadbolt on the back door, and I make sure his medication organizer is pushed all the way back on the shelf where no one can tamper with it. I try to explain this to my family, that she's warning me or testing me, but they just roll their eyes. But I hear her, doctor. I'm not making it up.",
